#d	Khmer–English, as complete as open sources make it: every Khmer word they give English for, with all they say of it. Its English words, all that correspond to it, commonest first, by sense where Chinese and Thai tell them apart (the Bible, ALT, FLORES-200 and OPUS’s subtitles, TED talks and KDE by the same English; a sense named by its Chinese, its meanings CC-CEDICT’s and Wiktionary’s, and its Thai): machine-made (Bibles, OPUS and NLLB word-aligned with eflomal), with people’s translations (Wiktionary’s, SBBIC’s); <a href="https://en.wiktionary.org/">English Wiktionary</a>’s entry and translation tables (via <a href="https://kaikki.org/">Kaikki</a>); <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/">Wikidata</a> (CC0); the Greek and Hebrew words the Khmer Bibles translate by it (STEPBible’s tagged texts, CC BY 4.0); its parts under their sources’ licences: <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> (Wiktionary’s), CC0 (Wikidata’s). Machine-made parts unchecked: verify before relying on them.
